\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite restricted products}

\p{Let #{G_p} be a Hausdorff locally compact topological group at
each rational prime, and choose a compact open subgroup #{U_p\leq G_p}. The
\newvocab{restricted product} relative to #{U=(U_p)} is

##{G_f(U)=\prod_p'(G_p,U_p).}

It consists of the tuples #{(g_p)\in\prod_pG_p} for which
#{g_p\in U_p} at all but finitely many primes.

For a finite set #{S} of primes its \newvocab{principal stage} is

##{G_S(U)=\prod_{p\in S}G_p\times\prod_{p\notin S}U_p,}

regarded as a subset of #{G_f(U)} and given the product topology.
The \newvocab{restricted-product topology} declares #{A\subseteq G_f(U)}
open exactly when #{A\cap G_S(U)} is open in every principal stage.
Multiplication and inversion are coordinatewise. The
\newvocab{product reference subgroup} is

##{K(U)=\prod_pU_p=G_{\varnothing}(U).}}

\p{For example, take the hyperbolic plane #{Q(x,y)=xy} and the
references from its standard rational basis in \ref{fcap-004D}.
The tuple whose #{2}-component is #{\operatorname{diag}(2,1/2)}
and whose other components are identities belongs to #{G_{\{2\}}(U)}
for #{G_p=O_p}, but not to #{K(U)}. The finite exceptional set
permits a nonintegral component without discarding the restrictions
at all other primes.}

\p{With this topology, #{G_f(U)} is a Hausdorff locally compact
topological group, each #{G_S(U)} is an open subspace with precisely
its product topology, and #{K(U)} is compact and open. Every
coordinate evaluation #{(g_q)\mapsto g_p} is continuous.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{The identity belongs to every reference subgroup. The product
of tuples with exceptional sets #{S} and #{T} has exceptional set
contained in #{S\cup T}; inversion preserves a tuple's exceptional
set. Thus the coordinate operations define a group on #{G_f(U)},
which is the union of its principal stages.}

\p{If #{S\subseteq T}, the inclusion #{G_S(U)\to G_T(U)}
replaces only the finitely many factors #{U_p} for #{p\in T\setminus S}
by #{G_p}. Each inclusion #{U_p\hookrightarrow G_p} is an open
embedding, so the product inclusion is an open embedding. For
arbitrary finite #{S,T},

##{G_S(U)\cap G_T(U)=G_{S\cap T}(U).}

This intersection is open in both stages with the same topology.
If #{A} is open in #{G_S(U)}, its intersection with #{G_T(U)}
is therefore open in #{G_T(U)}: first intersect with the open
#{G_{S\cap T}(U)}, then use its open inclusion into #{G_T(U)}.
Hence #{A} is open in the union topology. Conversely, an open set
of the union restricts to an open set in each stage by definition.
This proves both the claimed stage topology and stage openness.}

\p{A coordinate evaluation is continuous on every stage, hence on
the union by the definition of its topology. Two distinct tuples
differ at some prime; disjoint neighborhoods in that Hausdorff
factor pull back to disjoint neighborhoods of the tuples. Thus the
union is Hausdorff. Multiplication restricts to a continuous map

##{G_S(U)\times G_T(U)\longrightarrow G_{S\cup T}(U)}

by continuity in each factor of the product topology. These open
products cover #{G_f(U)\times G_f(U)}, proving continuity of
multiplication. Inversion is continuous on each open stage, hence
on the union. Finally #{K(U)} is compact by the product compactness
theorem and open as the empty stage. Its translates are compact
neighborhoods of every point, proving local compactness.}
}

\p{For a \vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C},
apply this construction to the three local families to define the
\newvocab{finite adelic orthogonal, special orthogonal and Spin groups}:

##{\begin{gathered}
O_f(U)=\prod_p'(O_p,U_p^O),\\
SO_f(U)=\prod_p'(SO_p,U_p^{SO}),\\
\Spin_f(U)=\prod_p'(\Spin_p,U_p^{\Spin}).
\end{gathered}}

The local topological groups are supplied by \ref{fcap-0045}.
The construction applies to the derived SO references as well as
to the chosen O and Spin references. In dimension zero all three
restricted products are singleton groups. Here the notation retains
the chosen tuple; an arbitrary compatible tuple has not been
identified with the integral points of an affine scheme.}

\p{Finite changes of references leave the ambient group and its
topology unchanged. More precisely, suppose #{U'_p=U_p} outside a
finite set #{F}. A tuple restricted for one family is restricted
for the other after adjoining #{F} to its exceptions. The identity
in every coordinate is thus a group isomorphism
#{G_f(U)\to G_f(U')}. On a stage it factors as

##{G_S(U)\longrightarrow G_{S\cup F}(U')\longrightarrow G_f(U').}

The first map is continuous: outside #{S\cup F} the reference
factors coincide, and at the finitely many remaining factors it
is either the identity on #{G_p} or the inclusion #{U_p\hookrightarrow G_p}.
Stagewise continuity gives continuity on the union. Interchanging
#{U} and #{U'} proves continuity of the inverse. Consequently
the changes in \ref{fcap-004E} identify the finite adelic topological
groups by the coordinate identity.}

\p{This identity need not send #{K(U)} onto #{K(U')}.
If, for example, #{U'_{p_0}} is a proper subgroup of #{U_{p_0}},
choose #{u\in U_{p_0}\setminus U'_{p_0}}. The tuple with component
#{u} at #{p_0} and identities elsewhere belongs to #{K(U)} but
not to #{K(U')}. Thus the ambient topology and the chosen compact
open subgroup are separate data. Continuity of all coordinate
evaluations also shows that the restricted-product topology is at
least as fine as the topology inherited from #{\prod_pG_p}.}

\p{The classical affine adelic construction uses finite stages and
open transition maps in \citet{Theorem 3.6, pp. 6-8}{conrad2011adelic};
compactness of integral points and the resulting locally compact
topology occur in \citet{Corollary 3.7 and Section 4 opening, p. 9}{conrad2011adelic}.
The argument above proves the construction directly for the chosen
compact-open group references. Mathlib's
\leanref{RestrictedProduct.isOpenEmbedding_inclusion_principal}
and \leanref{RestrictedProduct.locallyCompactSpace_of_group}
support the stage and local compactness assertions. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicOrthogonal},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpecialOrthogonal}
and \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpin}
define the three specializations. The coordinate identity and its
continuity are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.restrictedProductCongr} and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.continuous_restrictedProductCongr}.}
